WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.950
The entire Marvel Cinematic Universe has pretty
much been built around the Infinity Stones.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.160
Often called "Infinity Gems" in the comics,
these mystic superpowered baubles have shown

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.270
up in almost every Marvel Cinematic Universe
film to date — and it's all been leading

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:14.600
to Infinity War.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.470
These powerful little rocks have the power
to completely destroy the entire Marvel Cinematic

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:22.780
Universe, and Marvel fans have seen big bad
Thanos plotting to get his hands on them since

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.400
the first Avengers film.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.410
Everything climaxes in Avengers: Infinity
War, when he shows up to just take them all

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.070
for himself, as the comics predicted.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.980
But where exactly are the Infinity Stones
in the MCU?

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:37.090
We've encountered most of them already, and
it isn't hard to surmise where in the Marvel

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.260
Universe the rest might be scattered.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.290
Here's the full rundown of where we can find
all of the MCU's Infinity Stones leading up

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.570
to Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.219
The Mind Stone

00:00:47.219 --> 00:00:51.360
Loki used the Mind Stone in his scepter to
great effect in The Avengers, brainwashing

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.260
folks to do his bidding along the way.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.620
Earth's Mightiest Heroes spent the early part
of Avengers: Age of Ultron tracking it down

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:58.920
from HYDRA's control.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.239
Ultron stole it, embedded it in his potential
new body, but his plans went south when that

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:04.949
body was stolen by the good guys.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:09.270
Finally, team Stark tapped into its power
to activate the powerful new hero Vision to

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.260
help defeat Ultron.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.260
"It's the Mind Stone.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.270
It's one of the six Infinity Stones.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:17.040
The greatest powers in the universe unparalleled
in its destructive capabilities."

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.130
One side effect of that creation process?

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.420
The Mind Stone is stuck in Vision's forehead.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.480
It's what gives him his immense power, and
there's no way to get it out without something

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.930
really, really bad happening.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:31.320
When last we left Vision at the end of 2016's
Captain America: Civil War, he was serving

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.430
on the "official" Avengers squad under the
leadership of Iron Man.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.259
It stands to reason this will be one of the
easiest Stones for Thanos to find once he

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:42.290
lands in the middle of New York City, since
it'll be attached to a fist that will be punching

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.290
him in the head.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.979
If you need more than that, footage from the
film's first trailer shows one of Thanos'

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:49.850
minions making a move to pluck it out of Vision's
skull.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.250
No word on exactly what that will mean for
Vision, but it's probably not good news.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.950
The Space Stone

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.189
The Space Stone has been kicking around the
MCU since Captain America: The First Avenger,

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:04.350
when it was being called the Tesseract, and
has been used by both the Red Skull and Loki

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.979
in their attempts at world domination.

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:10.379
S.H.I.E.L.D. also held it for a while to try
and mine its capabilities for super-weapons.

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:14.620
After Thor, Iron Man and the gang defeated
Loki in The Avengers, the Tesseract was shipped

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.140
back to Asgard for safekeeping inside Odin's
vault, which housed all kinds of powerful,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.329
but possibly inauthentic, goodies.

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:23.700
"That's not bad."

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.370
That was the last place this particular Infinity
Stone was seen, and even though Asgard was

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:33.000
destroyed down to its foundations during Thor:
Ragnarok, it stands to reason it survived.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Best guess?

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.029
Loki was the last person in the vault, so
he almost certainly grabbed it before sparking

00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:40.829
the match to burn Asgard to the ground on
Thor's order.

00:02:40.829 --> 00:02:44.999
The first trailer for Infinity War offers
its own clues, showing Loki holding the Tesseract

00:02:44.999 --> 00:02:49.120
up for someone — so if nothing else, we
know he ends up with it somehow.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.689
The Time Stone

00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:55.760
The Time Stone was first introduced to the
MCU in Doctor Strange as the Eye of Agamotto,

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.139
discovered among the artifacts being held
by the Masters of the Mystic Arts.

00:02:59.139 --> 00:03:03.939
Strange uses it to create a time loop to save
the world from the reality-devouring Dormammu,

00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:07.370
outsmarting one of the Marvel Universe's most
dangerous entities.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.790
But the stone itself isn't without its dangers.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:13.159
"Temporal manipulations can create branches
in time.

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:17.900
Unstable dimensional openings, spatial paradoxes,
time loops!"

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.370
Despite the fact that he saved the world,
Strange is still learning the ropes when it

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.269
comes to the mystic arts, so the Time Stone
was put back in the hands of the Masters of

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:29.569
the Mystic Arts and is likely being stored
at Kamar-Taj in Kathmandu, Nepal.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:33.639
When Thanos inevitably attacks, it would make
sense for him to either come after the Stone.

00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:37.779
Perhaps Doctor Strange will arm up with the
Eye as he heads into battle with the Mad Titan.

00:03:37.779 --> 00:03:41.659
Regardless, like the Mind Stone, that Infinity
Stone is already on Earth and should make

00:03:41.659 --> 00:03:44.049
for an easy target for Thanos.

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:45.769
The Reality Stone

00:03:45.769 --> 00:03:49.400
The chain of custody on the Reality Stone
is a bit harder to track than the rest of

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.290
the MCU's Infinity Stones.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:55.139
First introduced in Thor: The Dark World as
the extremely powerful Aether sought after

00:03:55.139 --> 00:03:59.529
by Malekith, the Reality Stone was eventually
recovered by Thor by the end of the film once

00:03:59.529 --> 00:04:01.489
the Dark Elves were vanquished.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:05.129
After that, the pmid-credits scene in The
Dark World revealed the Asgardians decided

00:04:05.129 --> 00:04:08.980
to hand the Stone off to the Collector for
safekeeping, because of the dangers of keeping

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.430
two stones in the same place.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:17.329
"One down, five to go."

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:21.430
The Collector's home on Knowhere was eventually
destroyed in Guardians of the Galaxy, though

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.330
it stands to reason he dug the Reality Stone
out of the rubble and is still in possession

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:26.560
of it at this point.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.960
That's assuming he hasn't simply sold it off
to Thanos in the meantime, of course.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.800
The Power Stone

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.919
Most Marvel movies revolve around powerful
MacGuffins, but few are as powerful as the

00:04:36.919 --> 00:04:40.479
mysterious orb everyone is fighting over in
the first Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:44.760
That world-destroying orb is the Power Stone,
which Peter Quill tracks down on a planet

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.479
during the film's opening scenes.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:50.199
Ronan the Accuser later attempts to use it
to wipe Xandar off the map.

00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:53.889
Quill eventually reacquires it, and holds
hands with the rest of his team, and uses

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:56.320
its power to take out Ronan once and for all.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.270
Once the battle was won, Quill handed the
Power Stone over to the Nova Corps for safekeeping

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.270
on Xandar.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.789
Presuming it hasn't wandered off to another
spot in the MCU, it Thanos will probably have

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:09.259
to wage war against the entire intergalactic
police force to get his hands on this particular

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:11.080
Infinity Stone.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.710
The Soul Stone

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:16.669
The first few phases of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe have done most of the heavy lifting

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:21.669
introducing the majority of the Infinity Stones,
but one mystery remains: The Soul Stone.

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:26.300
The mysterious final stone has yet to be formally
introduced in the MCU — though there are

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.300
a few theories.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.490
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 has already
planted the seed for Adam Warlock's introduction,

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.360
and the Soul Stone is intimately connected
to his origin story in the comics.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.440
So if Marvel wanted to bring that character
into the fold ahead of Guardians Volume 3,

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.199
it'd make for a convenient outlet.

00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:43.939
The most likely option, though, seems to be
Black Panther.

00:05:43.939 --> 00:05:48.090
The land of Wakanda is the most technologically
advanced on the planet, and is an extremely

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:52.319
spiritual place, to the point we see Black
Panther actually travel to the spirit realm

00:05:52.319 --> 00:05:53.970
to confer with his late father.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:58.639
Looking to the footage we've seen of Infinity
War, Wakanda seems to factor in heavily — to

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:01.629
the point we see a full Wakandan army charging
into battle.

00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:05.789
Could Wakanda be in possession of the final
Infinity Stone, and that charge is the nation's

00:06:05.789 --> 00:06:07.990
battle to keep it from falling into Thanos'
hands?

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:13.039
It'd make a lot of sense, and give Black Panther
real stakes in Thanos' assault.

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:14.039
The Gauntlet

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:18.879
All of the MCU's Infinity Stones have to fit
together somehow, and that's where the Infinity

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:20.129
Gauntlet comes in.

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:24.280
It's designed to hold them all and give the
wearer practically godlike powers, and Thanos

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:29.090
has a leg up on the competition: The post-credits
scene of Avengers: Age of Ultron showed Thanos

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:33.060
wearing the Stone-less gauntlet; he only needs
to gather the Stones when Infinity War rolls

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.060
around.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:38.199
As keen-eyed fans are likely also aware, the
Infinity Gauntlet was previously shown all

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:42.560
the way back in 2011's Thor, when it was featured
in the background of Odin's vault.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.069
It created a continuity problem once things
started barreling toward an eventual war with

00:06:47.069 --> 00:06:50.580
Thanos, so Thor: Ragnarok cleverly retconned
that Gauntlet.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:51.699
"Odin's treasures."

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.699
"FAKE.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.259
Most of the stuff in here is fake."

00:06:56.259 --> 00:07:00.479
It made for a fun joke, but also cleaned up
a messy plot thread that had been left dangling

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:02.430
since 2011.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.270
Infinity Stones assembled

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:08.841
Promo art, action figures, and early footage
from Avengers: Infinity War shows Thanos in

00:07:08.841 --> 00:07:13.699
possession of all the Infinity Stones in the
MCU, which doesn't bode well for Earth's Mightiest

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:14.699
Heroes.

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:18.759
If the MCU Gauntlet has a power set similar
to the one in the comics, all the heroes in

00:07:18.759 --> 00:07:21.510
the MCU will have their hands full stopping
Thanos.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:26.050
It allows the wearer to manipulate time and
change the past, manipulate space and matter,

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:28.411
and basically rewrite reality itself on a
whim.

00:07:28.411 --> 00:07:29.411
The comics version of the gauntlet, when fully
powered, can also give the wearer godlike

00:07:29.411 --> 00:07:30.411
omnipotence.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.599
Which means Thanos is about to make the Avengers
and their friends' lives very, very difficult.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:35.100
"Ready?"

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:36.060
"Let's go."

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:36.960
Thanks for watching!

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.279
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:40.279 --> 00:07:42.499
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

